<interrogationes unitas="stml">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I parse an STML document and tell whether it succeeded?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_legere stml_legere_ex_literis StmlResultus">Call
      stml_legere(input, piscina, intern) (or stml_legere_ex_literis for
      a NUL-terminated C string). It returns an StmlResultus by value:
      check successus (status == STML_SUCCESSUS). radix is the DOCUMENTUM
      node and elementum_radix its first element child. On failure
      status names the class, linea_erroris and columna_erroris locate
      the FIRST error (later ones are treated as its cascade). The
      error chorda is filled only by the named refusals (MULTILINEA and
      the attribute-element ATTRIBUTUM ones); most errors, including a
      single-quoted value (ATTRIBUTUM), leave it empty. radix is still
      set after a parse error (a partial tree), so test successus, not
      radix; empty input gives STML_ERROR_VACUUM_INPUT with radix NIHIL.
      CRLF is turned into LF once on entry (crlf_canonicalizatum says
      so). Caution (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed): some malformed inputs still give successus
      with a silently corrupted tree (a closer missing '&gt;' as in
      &lt;a&gt;t&lt;/a, a non-name byte in a tag name as in
      &lt;a+b&gt;x&lt;/a+b&gt;, the fake doctype
      &lt;!D&gt;&lt;a/&gt;b&gt;&lt;c/&gt;, capture operators missing
      '=&gt;' or '&gt;'), and nesting about 100000 levels deep kills the
      process with SIGSEGV instead of returning an error.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I empty the piscina I parsed into and keep the strings I read from the tree?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_legere chorda_internare internamentum_creare piscina_vacare chorda_ut_cstr" via="lib/agenda.c">Yes
      for the strings, no for the nodes. Nodes, their Xars, the error
      chorda and the results of the text accessors live in the piscina
      you pass in. Every titulus, valor, attribute value and trivia
      chorda is interned, and an InternamentumChorda copies into its OWN
      piscina (the one given to internamentum_creare), so those chordae
      survive piscina_vacare on the parse piscina (agenda.c reparses
      into a scratch piscina this way). They are chorda, not C strings:
      not NUL-terminated, so compare with chorda_aequalis_literis and
      convert with chorda_ut_cstr.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I read an attribute, and how do I tell a boolean attribute from a missing one?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_attributum_capere stml_attributum_habet stml_strictum">stml_attributum_capere(nodus,
      "name") returns the interned chorda* or NIHIL when absent. A
      boolean attribute (&lt;a x&gt;) stores the interned value "true",
      so &lt;a x&gt; and &lt;a x="true"&gt; are the same thing and the
      writer emits both as bare x, in both modes. Values are RAW: no
      entity decoding. With a duplicated attribute the FIRST one wins
      (stml_strictum reports it). If no inline attribute matches, capere
      also looks at attribute ELEMENTS (&lt;@name=&gt;text&lt;/&gt;) at
      the start of the children and returns their text; an empty one is
      a tombstone and reads as NIHIL. stml_attributum_habet is just
      capere != NIHIL, so an attribute written v="" also reads as
      absent (see the next question).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I store an empty string or a double quote in an attribute value?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_attributum_addere stml_attributum_capere chorda_internare stml_scribere stml_elementum_creare" via="lib/agenda.c lib/personae.c">Neither
      works inline. An empty chorda is never interned (chorda_internare
      returns NIHIL), so &lt;a v=""/&gt; parses with valor NIHIL:
      stml_attributum_capere returns NIHIL as if v were absent, and the
      writer emits bare v, which reads back as "true". A '"' cannot be
      written because inline values are raw and always wrapped in '"';
      stml_attributum_addere does not check, and what stml_scribere
      emits either fails to parse or silently re-splits
      (v="x"y" reads back as v="x" plus a boolean y). For a value with
      '"' use an attribute element &lt;@name=&gt;text&lt;/&gt; (its
      text child decodes entities). No constructor exists: like
      titulum_addere in lib/agenda.c or nomen_addere in lib/personae.c,
      call stml_elementum_creare(p, in, "@"), set attributum_titulus to
      the interned name, give it one text child, and place it before
      every other child. Only that leading run is read as attributes,
      and the parser refuses an attribute element that comes after any
      other child (a comment counts) unless it follows a template call
      &lt;&lt;#@...&gt;&gt;; nothing checks this when you build or write
      the tree.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I find elements by tag name, or loop over all children of a node?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_invenire_liberum stml_invenire_omnes_liberos stml_quaerere stml_quaerere_omnes stml_numerus_liberorum stml_liberum_ad_indicem stml_frater_proximus">stml_invenire_liberum
      and stml_invenire_omnes_liberos look at DIRECT children only and
      match element nodes by exact name. For descendants or CSS-style
      selectors (tag, #id, .label, [attr=val], &gt;, +, ~,
      :first-child, :last-child, :empty) use stml_quaerere /
      stml_quaerere_omnes from selectio.h. To walk children, loop i over
      stml_numerus_liberorum with stml_liberum_ad_indicem and check
      genus: text, comment and element nodes are mixed, and attribute
      elements (&lt;@name=&gt;) are element children too, with titulus
      "@". Avoid stml_frater_proximus in a loop: each call rescans the
      parent's children to find its own index.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I get the text inside an element, and which of the four text accessors should I use?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_textus_valor stml_textus_fluxus stml_textus_normalizatus stml_textus_internus" via="project-specs/stml-trivia-spec.md lib/stml.worklog.md">Use
      stml_textus_valor for data: the text nodes concatenated in order,
      comments and layout trivia excluded. It is stable under the
      fidelity writer and for raw or '\' content, but the pretty writer
      re-wraps flow prose, which changes the newlines inside a text
      value. stml_textus_fluxus reads prose: a whitespace run that holds
      a newline becomes one space, so it survives that re-wrap.
      stml_textus_normalizatus removes common indentation and
      leading/trailing blank lines from hand-indented blocks.
      stml_textus_internus rebuilds the layout bytes (trivia) as well;
      despite the header's wording, entities come back DECODED
      (x&amp;amp;y reads x&amp;y). All four allocate in the piscina you
      pass and return a chorda that is not NUL-terminated. Caution (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed): the
      pretty writer drops a newline-bearing whitespace run after an
      inline child element or comment, so
      &lt;p&gt;one &lt;b&gt;two&lt;/b&gt;\n  three&lt;/p&gt; reads back
      with fluxus "one twothree" and &lt;p&gt;a&lt;!--c--&gt;\n
      b&lt;/p&gt; with "ab".</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I write a tree back to text, and what is the difference between pretty and non-pretty output?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_scribere stml_scribere_ad_aedificator stml_scribere_sedibus" via="project-specs/stml-trivia-spec.md">stml_scribere(nodus,
      piscina, pulchrum). With pulchrum FALSUM it re-emits the stored
      trivia, so a parsed document round-trips byte for byte over the
      LF-canonicalized input, apart from attributes: spaces around '='
      are dropped, unquoted values gain quotes, x="true" becomes bare x
      and v="" becomes bare v. With pulchrum VERUM it is a reformatter:
      trivia are ignored and layout is regenerated (two-space indent,
      capture forms, prose re-filled to LXXII columns, wider when deeply
      nested). A tree built in code has no trivia, so FALSUM writes it
      on one line; use VERUM. stml_scribere_ad_aedificator appends to a
      ChordaAedificator, and stml_scribere_sedibus also records where
      each element landed in the output. Caution (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed): pulchrum VERUM
      loses the space where a newline follows an inline child
      (&lt;p&gt;one &lt;b&gt;two&lt;/b&gt;\n  three&lt;/p&gt; is written
      &lt;p&gt;one &lt;b&gt;two&lt;/b&gt;three&lt;/&gt;), and a tree
      nested about 100000 deep crashes stml_scribere with
      SIGSEGV.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does the pretty writer emit </> and <a(> forms instead of named closing tags?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_scribere" via="lib/stml.worklog.md">The
      pretty-layout decrees (worklog 2026-08-19 to 2026-08-25). A
      repeated name is noise while the open tag is still on screen, so
      the closer is the anonymous &lt;/&gt; until the content passes
      XXX lines (STML_CLAUSURA_TACITA_LINEAE). This cannot oscillate
      because both closers take the same number of lines. Eligible
      single-child chains collapse to capture form (&lt;a(&gt;
      &lt;b(&gt; text), and elements whose two or three children are
      all simple elements, within XII lines, use &lt;t((&gt; /
      &lt;t(((&gt; with the children stacked, so closing tags remain
      only on the multi-child block form. Raw elements always keep
      their named closer, and attribute elements always close with
      &lt;/&gt;. Non-pretty output keeps the author's own
      form.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what are the capture operators <tag (>, <) tag>, <= tag => and <tag! (>?
    <responsum symbolum="StmlCaptioDirectio stml_legere">Ways
      to give an element children without a closing tag; they are
      resolved after parsing. &lt;tag (&gt; takes the next N
      non-comment siblings, one per paren (chains such as
      &lt;a(&gt; &lt;b(&gt; x nest), and comments in between become its
      children too. &lt;) tag&gt; takes the previous N (comments in
      between stay siblings), &lt;= tag =&gt; one before and one after.
      &lt;tag! (&gt; captures the rest of the LINE raw (no tags, no
      entities, newline excluded). Fragments capture too (&lt;#id
      (&gt;, sugar &lt;(&gt;). The node records captio_directio and
      captio_numerus. Known gap: a comment between &lt;= tag =&gt; and
      its following captee is dropped. Caution (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed): STML_ERROR_CAPTIO
      is never set, so a malformed operator (&lt;r&gt;x &lt;= s
      y&lt;/r&gt; with no '=&gt;', &lt;) z with no '&gt;') or a captor
      with too few siblings (&lt;r&gt;&lt;a (&gt;&lt;/r&gt;) parses with
      status 0 and the writer invents the missing terminator.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can a raw <tag!> element contain its own closing sequence </tag>?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_crudi_sequentia_est stml_crudi_terminator_est stml_crudi_terminatorem_fert">Yes,
      through the escape ladder. A raw region ends at &lt;/T followed by
      &gt;, ! or whitespace. The writer escapes such a sequence in the
      value by adding a backslash (&lt;\/T&gt;), and one more for each
      level already present; the reader removes one. Scanner and writer
      share one definition (stml_crudi_sequentia_est), and
      stml_crudi_terminatorem_fert tells you whether a value carries a
      terminator. One limit: a raw value that STARTS with &lt;/T
      followed by a name character (&lt;code!&gt;&lt;/codex...) is not
      escaped and fails to parse, because the check at the very start
      of raw content compares only the &lt;/T prefix.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I build an element in code with attributes, text and children?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_elementum_creare stml_attributum_addere stml_attributum_boolean_addere stml_textum_addere stml_liberum_addere stml_praeponere stml_duplicare_superficialiter">stml_elementum_creare
      gives an element with empty attributa and liberi Xars; then
      stml_attributum_addere (or _chorda, _boolean_addere),
      stml_textum_addere and stml_liberum_addere. Names and values are
      interned, so the C strings can be temporary. The add functions
      return FALSUM when the target Xar is NIHIL, as on any node from
      stml_duplicare_superficialiter (liberi is always NIHIL), on
      stml_duplicare copies of nodes that had no children or no
      attributes, and on parsed &lt;#x/&gt; fragments. stml_praeponere
      creates a missing liberi; nothing creates a missing
      attributa. Caution (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed): no name is validated, so
      stml_elementum_creare with "a b", "", "9bad" or "-x" and
      stml_attributum_addere with ".k", a NIHIL titulus or a NIHIL
      valor all succeed, yet the written tree reads back changed or
      fails (titulus "" is written &lt;/&gt; and closes the parent; a
      NIHIL valor is written as bare k while capere on the built node
      returns NIHIL).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I move a node to another parent with stml_liberum_addere or stml_inserere_post?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_removere stml_liberum_addere stml_inserere_ante stml_inserere_post stml_substituere">Only
      after detaching it. No insert function (nor stml_substituere for
      its new node) removes the node from its old parent: it overwrites
      parens, the old parent still lists the node, and it is written
      twice. Call stml_removere first (it finds the parent through
      parens). stml_praeponere, stml_inserere_ante, stml_inserere_post
      and stml_removere build a NEW liberi Xar on every call (O(n),
      allocated from the piscina), so a pointer to the old
      parent-&gt;liberi goes stale; stml_liberum_addere and
      stml_substituere work in place. Nodes made with the creare
      functions carry no trivia, so non-pretty output glues them to
      their neighbours; moved or cloned parsed nodes keep their
      own.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I map byte positions in STML text to tokens or nodes, for highlighting or jump-to?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_lexemata_colligere StmlLexema StmlNodus stml_scribere_sedibus StmlSedesNodi">For
      tokens, stml_lexemata_colligere returns a Xar of StmlLexema
      (genus, valor, initium/finis byte offsets [inclusive, exclusive),
      1-based linea and byte columna) for ANY input, even half-typed
      text, with no parse and no error; it returns NIHIL for empty
      input or a NIHIL piscina/intern, and STML_TOKEN_FINIS is not
      added. Paint by the extent, not by valor (text token valor is the
      raw source slice). For parsed nodes, positus_initium and
      positus_finis span the opening tag through the closing tag, and
      linea is the start line; they are ZEPHYRUM on built nodes, and a
      captor's span covers only its own tag. All these offsets refer to
      the CRLF-to-LF canonicalized text. For written output,
      stml_scribere_sedibus fills a caller-made Xar of StmlSedesNodi for
      elements only, in post-order.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does stml_strictum check that stml_legere lets through?
    <responsum symbolum="stml_strictum stml_strictum_nuntius StmlStrictumGenus">The
      parser is deliberately lenient; stml_strictum(result.radix,
      piscina) judges the parsed DOCUMENT node and returns a Xar of
      StmlStrictumVitium: duplicated inline attribute, more than one
      root element, non-whitespace text outside the root, and a nameless
      element (&lt; &gt;, with a space; &lt;&gt; is an anonymous
      fragment). stml_strictum_nuntius gives the message. Accepted on
      purpose: unknown entities stay literal text (only &amp;lt;
      &amp;gt; &amp;amp; &amp;quot; &amp;apos; decode, so &amp;#60; is
      kept as written), and unquoted values. Unquoted values may only
      use letters, digits, '-' and '_': &lt;a v=1.5&gt; reads as v="1"
      and the rest of the tag becomes text, which stml_strictum does
      not catch.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
